        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "8ed663c8-f784-5b76-b040-90ff391a85e5",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: A. J. Watson, Rodger Ward\n- Organizations: United States Air Force, USAC\n- Places: Pocono International Raceway, Trenton, Indianapolis, Milwaukee\n- Topics: Open-wheel racing, Indianapolis 500, USAC National Championship\n\nFacts:\n- Rodger Ward was born on January 10, 1921, and he later served as a World War II P-38 aviator in the United States Air Force.\n- Rodger Ward became an American race driver who earned 26 victories in top-echelon open-wheel racing in North America.\n- Rodger Ward won two Indianapolis 500 races and two USAC National Championships.\n- Rodger Ward conceived the classic tri-oval design and layout of Pocono International Raceway, modeled after turns at Trenton, Indianapolis, and Milwaukee.\n- In 1962, Rodger Ward was the USAC National Champion and the Indianapolis 500 winner.\n- Rodger Ward won 18 races driving cars built by A. J. Watson.\n- Rodger Ward died on July 5, 2004.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
        "id": 1,
